# Let Git identify text versus binary files, and use LF for text files.
* text=auto eol=lf

# Windows command scripts should retain CRLF.
*.bat text eol=crlf
*.cmd text eol=crlf


#The effect is:
#Git repository/index:
#    text normalized to LF
#
#Working tree:
#    ordinary text files → LF
#    .bat and .cmd       → CRLF
#    binary files        → unchanged
#
# This also makes the repository’s behaviour less dependent on each developer’s global core.autocrlf setting.
#
# Is LF okay on native Windows?
# Yes, for:
#	- Python.
#	- VS Code.
#	- Git Bash.
#	- TOML/JSON/YAML.
#	- Markdown.
#	- Modern PowerShell scripts in ordinary UTF-8 projects.
# Add an explicit exception later if a particular Windows-only program requires CRLF.
#
# Existing repository warning
# If the repository already contains tracked files, adding .gitattributes may make Git see many line-ending changes. After adding the file, the standard normalization command is:
#		"git add --renormalize ."
#Then inspect before committing:
#		"git status"
#		"git diff --cached"
#
# For a brand-new repository, place .gitattributes before the first substantive commit and no special migration is needed.

# Above has the effect:
# -----------------------------------------------
# File                             	Line ending
# -----------------------------------------------
# .js, .ts, .json, .md, .ps1, etc.	LF
# .bat, .cmd                      	CRLF
# -----------------------------------------------
# NOTE: Modern Windows PowerShell and PowerShell 7 can execute .ps1 files using LF.